1
00:00:00,600 --> 00:00:09,700
Picture a pair of developers switching on the same AI coding assistant
on the same morning: a veteran, and a newcomer just starting out.

2
00:00:09,700 --> 00:00:11,520
Which of them gets more out of it?

3
00:00:12,033 --> 00:00:18,296
The confident answer online is the veteran, so let's put
that answer next to the trials that actually measured it.

4
00:00:18,300 --> 00:00:29,125
On The Pragmatic Engineer, in an episode about AI at Shopify, it was offered as a maybe:
"the AI agents are going to help the best engineers more than the mediocre engineer".

5
00:00:29,133 --> 00:00:37,358
On the same show, Simon Willison said that with these tools he will "run
circles around somebody who's never written a code line of code in their life".

6
00:00:37,367 --> 00:00:51,504
Willison's yardstick is someone who has never coded, and the trials that follow compared developers with each other, so what they
can test is the first claim: when researchers split their results by experience, did the best engineers gain more than the rest?

7
00:00:52,033 --> 00:01:04,458
In field experiments that gave developers the coding assistant Copilot, short-tenure developers
raised their output by 27 to 39 percent, while long-tenure developers gained 8 to 13 percent.

8
00:01:04,767 --> 00:01:14,004
Those estimates are noisy and not statistically significant, but the
pattern was the same on all three of the studies' main output measures.

9
00:01:14,033 --> 00:01:27,621
The authors note that longer-tenure developers abandoning the tool after a trial may shrink their estimate, yet
junior developers, who used it as much as seniors once they had adopted it, still gained more than senior ones.

10
00:01:27,633 --> 00:01:37,958
A separate experiment found the same direction: developers with fewer years of experience
got the speed benefit from generative AI, while the quality of their code was unaffected.

11
00:01:38,267 --> 00:01:50,154
Another Copilot trial found that less experienced programmers benefited more, and its authors called
that promising for people moving into software development, if the result holds up in further studies.

12
00:01:50,167 --> 00:02:04,004
An experiment that ran from bachelor's students to professional developers found that every group got significantly further
through its task with AI support, and its authors say the differences between groups were not related to experience.

13
00:02:04,333 --> 00:02:13,708
Professionals gained more there than bachelor's students, but each group had
tasks suited to its level, and the professionals' were among the most complex.

14
00:02:13,733 --> 00:02:23,171
Then there is a trial of developers who knew their projects deeply: 5
years and 1,500 commits on average in the repositories they worked on.

15
00:02:23,200 --> 00:02:41,475
With AI allowed, these developers were slower, not faster, and its researchers report moderate evidence that they were slowed down more on issues where
they had high prior exposure to the task; the developers said AI was particularly helpful on unfamiliar issues and less helpful on familiar ones.

16
00:02:41,500 --> 00:02:50,525
As one developer put it: "if I am the dedicated maintainer of a very specialized
part of the codebase, there is no way agent mode can do better than me."

17
00:02:51,033 --> 00:03:02,608
One trial leaned the other way: on a complex task, more senior engineers were, if anything,
faster with AI than less senior ones, though that difference was not statistically significant.

18
00:03:02,933 --> 00:03:09,521
Its authors suggest AI may not yet be able
to close a skill gap on a task that complex.

19
00:03:09,533 --> 00:03:16,696
That leaves the other half of the belief: that using these
tools well is a skill of its own, and the skilled pull ahead.

20
00:03:17,200 --> 00:03:28,288
Among those deeply experienced developers, the slowdown broadly did not shrink with up to
50 hours of Cursor experience, counting the hours they reported from before the study.

21
00:03:28,600 --> 00:03:41,688
One developer past 50 hours did see a speedup, but when a second developer corrected the experience
they had reported, that group's estimate fell from roughly 25 percent faster to roughly 0 percent.

22
00:03:41,700 --> 00:04:04,200
In the trial that leaned toward seniors, run with full-time Googlers, how often people already used AI tools made no significant difference to their speed with AI; among the possible
reasons its authors offer are too little statistical power, a low usability floor where the benefits come quickly, and one feature needing prompting skills that may not be universal yet.

23
00:04:04,200 --> 00:04:19,638
The clearest sign that tool skill matters comes from a maintainability trial: the more practised the original developer was with AI, the
better the code scored on a code-health measure once someone else built on it, though that developer's proficiency in Java mattered even more.

24
00:04:20,167 --> 00:04:39,367
So across these trials, nothing shows AI multiplying experts far more than novices: several leaned toward the less experienced, though the field experiments'
gaps were noisy and not significant and another trial's result awaits further studies, and the one hint the other way was not significant either.

25
00:04:39,667 --> 00:04:52,254
What runs through them is how much the developer already knew: the tool helped more for short-tenure developers
in one trial and on unfamiliar issues in another, and helped less for maintainers on tasks they knew well.

26
00:04:52,267 --> 00:04:56,879
But how can a tool that writes code for you
leave an expert slower on work they know best?

27
00:04:57,900 --> 00:05:07,275
Back with those maintainers, the answer starts with how often they took what
the tool offered: they accepted fewer than 44 percent of Cursor's generations.

28
00:05:07,600 --> 00:05:18,013
When they turned one down, they reprompted or gave up, and the researchers saw
significant time wasted reviewing, testing or modifying code before rejecting it.

29
00:05:18,033 --> 00:05:29,271
Even accepted code cost time: 75 percent said they read every line the AI
wrote, and 56 percent said they often had to make major changes to clean it up.

30
00:05:29,300 --> 00:05:39,325
Engineers deciding when to hand work to Claude described the same trade; for a
task under 10 minutes, one said, "I'm probably not going to bother using Claude."

31
00:05:39,633 --> 00:05:50,408
Another described a cold start: their team's codebase holds knowledge Claude does not have by
default, so rather than iterate on the perfect prompt, "I'm just going to go and do it myself."

32
00:05:50,433 --> 00:06:06,196
The same limit shows up at larger scale: in one company's AI-assisted migration of 5,359 files, the bottleneck was
how fast engineers could review the changes, so the team deliberately capped how many changes it generated each week.

33
00:06:06,200 --> 00:06:19,650
A review of what has been written about vibe coding names its central risk a verification gap: code
generated faster than developers and their processes can verify its correctness, security and provenance.

34
00:06:19,967 --> 00:06:27,729
Its authors suggest that skill in verification, constraint
specification and review may matter as much as skill in writing code.

35
00:06:27,733 --> 00:06:42,133
And the study of engineers using Claude names the catch, a "paradox of supervision": using Claude effectively
requires supervising it, and supervising it requires the very coding skills that may atrophy from overuse.

36
00:06:42,433 --> 00:06:45,771
That checking is part of the
price those maintainers paid.

37
00:06:46,800 --> 00:06:54,100
So before you hand a task to an assistant, ask yourself how much of
it you already know, and how long checking its answer will take.

38
00:06:54,400 --> 00:07:02,338
And if you find yourself deleting most of what it writes, point it at the parts
that are new to you, and keep enough of the skill to check what it hands back.
